repository, reproduced unmodified from upstream).

What is derived
---------------
The two remaining bricks arrive as git submodules under external/ and
carry their own NOTICE files (historical CTLL/CTRE lineage, the
ECMA-262 and W3C CSS semantics; compile-time-containers, MIT, supplies
the constexpr containers). This repository began as a clone of
compile-time-css - the chain runs compile-time-xml ->
compile-time-html -> compile-time-css -> here - and keeps that git
history. The DOM (its own WHATWG tokenizer and tree builder), the
style, layout, paint and raster pipeline, the script VM and standard
library, and the SDL3 shell are original to this repository.

The vendored fonts/ directory carries Tinos, Fira Sans and Cousine
from the Google Fonts collection (github.com/google/fonts), each
licensed under the SIL Open Font License 1.1 - see fonts/README.md
and the OFL texts there. They are loaded through the asset registry as
the default serif/sans-serif/monospace faces.

Attribution for the historical CTLL engine belongs to Hana Dusikova;
this file preserves it as required by Section 4(c) of the Apache
License 2.0 for the versions that shipped it.

The embedded 8x8 bitmap font (src/raster/font8x8.hpp) is
PUBLIC DOMAIN: font8x8 by Daniel Hepper <daniel@hepper.net>
(https://github.com/dhepper/font8x8), based on public-domain work by
Marcel Sondaar / International Business Machines.

The example assets (examples/assets/) are generated deterministically
by tools/gen-assets.py in this repository and carry no third-party
content, with two exceptions. The font
examples/assets/fonts/PressStart2P-Regular.ttf is "Press Start 2P" by
CodeMan38 (Cody Boisclair), used and redistributed under the SIL Open
Font License, Version 1.1 (the license text is alongside the font at
examples/assets/fonts/OFL.txt).

examples/assets/p5.js is p5.js v2.3.1 (https://p5js.org,
https://github.com/processing/p5.js), Copyright (c) the p5.js
contributors, redistributed UNMODIFIED under the GNU Lesser General
Public License, version 2.1. It is a distribution bundle and vendors
several MIT-licensed libraries inside itself - acorn, zod and others -
whose copyright notices are preserved inline in the file. It is test
DATA, not a dependency: nothing in the engine links it, includes it or
embeds it, and it is present so tests/p5_ratchet.cpp can measure how
much of a real, large, modern JavaScript library this engine can take.

Simple DirectMedia Layer (SDL3, https://libsdl.org) and its optional
satellite libraries SDL3_image, SDL3_mixer and SDL3_ttf are used as
external dependencies and are NOT bundled; all are distributed under
the zlib license.

plutosvg 0.0.8 (https://github.com/sammycage/plutosvg) and plutovg 1.3.3
(https://github.com/sammycage/plutovg), by Samuel Ugochukwu, are used as
external dependencies and are NOT bundled; both are distributed under
the MIT license. They render SVG - see include/ctbrowser/raster/svg.hpp -
and are optional: without them the engine builds and pages lay out
identically, they simply draw no vector graphics. The versions are
PINNED to the ones in the Windows cross-build sysroot, where SDL3_ttf
already links plutosvg for colour glyphs, so that one golden image
compares byte-for-byte on both platforms.

--- the engine (src/) ---

The engine uses Boost, HEADER-ONLY LIBRARIES ONLY (Boost Software
License 1.0, https://www.boost.org/LICENSE_1_0.txt), as an external
dependency; nothing from Boost is bundled and nothing is linked.
Currently used: Boost.Unordered (unordered_flat_map), Boost.Container
(small_vector, flat_map, stable_vector), Boost.Intrusive, Boost.SmartPtr
(intrusive_ptr) and Boost.Lockfree (spsc_queue).

The header-only restriction is load bearing, not a preference. The
`windows` / `windows-fetch` CMake presets cross-compile with llvm-mingw;
a header-only Boost drops into that build with no new machinery, while a
compiled Boost would have to be cross-compiled for every target. In
particular Boost.Context (and therefore Boost.Coroutine2) is per-ABI
assembly and MUST NOT be introduced.

The compositor's shaders (src/gpu/shaders/tile.vert.glsl and
tile.frag.glsl) are original to this project. src/gpu/shaders/tile_spv.hpp
is their SPIR-V, generated by tools/gen-shaders.py using glslc from
Google's shaderc, which is a build-time tool and is NOT bundled or
linked.
